bs/jerome-bonaparte.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Jérôme Bonaparte</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Jérôme Bonaparte was the youngest brother of Napoleon, who made him king of Westphalia from 1807 to 1813, with Kassel as his capital.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/jeronimo-de-aguilar"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/jeronimo-de-aguilar.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Jerónimo de Aguilar</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Jerónimo de Aguilar was a Spanish Franciscan who was shipwrecked on the coast of Yucatán in 1511 and lived among the Maya for eight years.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/jeronimo-de-albuquerque"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/jeronimo-de-albuquerque.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Jerónimo de Albuquerque</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Jerónimo de Albuquerque was a Portuguese nobleman in Brazil, the brother-in-law of Duarte Coelho, first donatary of the Captaincy of Pernambuco.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/jeronimo-de-albuquerque-maranhao"><span class="enc-name">Jerónimo de Albuquerque Maranhão</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Jerónimo de Albuquerque Maranhão was a Portuguese-Brazilian commander, son of Jerónimo de Albuquerque of Pernambuco and a Tabajara mother.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/jeronimos-monastery"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/jeronimos-monastery.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Jerónimos Monastery</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">The Jerónimos Monastery is a monastery in Lisbon built from the early sixteenth century in the ornate Manueline style.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/jerriais"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/jerriais.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Jèrriais</span><span class="enc-type">Term</span><span class="enc-snip">Jèrriais is the Norman language of Jersey, a variety of Norman French spoken on the island since the Middle Ages.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/jersey-royal"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/jersey-royal.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Jersey Royal</span><span class="enc-type">Term</span><span class="enc-snip">The Jersey Royal is a variety of early new potato grown in Jersey, developed around 1880 by the farmer Hugh de la Haye from an unusually large potato with many eyes.</span></a></li></ul>
